    <property name="_vergilRevision" class="ptolemy.kernel.util.ConfigurableAttribute">
        <configure>51</configure>
    </property>
    <doc name="Desc">This example illustrate the manifestation of Self Phase Modulation (SPM) and Parametric Gain &#10;as Modulation Instability (MI).&#10;&#10;The system is an 800Km single-channel link at 2.5 Gbit/s, which is severely limited by non-linear effects.&#10;&#10;The fiber is standard single mode with D approximately 16 ps/nm/km.&#10;The avarage launch power is 10 dBm and it is maintained along the link by 8 in-line amplifiers.&#10;&#10;By studying the received power spectrum it is seen that the signal spectrum is extremely distorted. &#10;Part of the impairment is SPM that, combined with the high fiber dispersion, brings about a large pulse &#10;distorsion in the eye diagram. Most of the impairment, however, is due to Modulation Instability (MI).&#10;&#10;MI is due to a phenomenon called Parametric Gain in the fiber that essentially enhances the ASE noise &#10;in the proximity of a powerful signal.&#10;In anomalous dispersion PG takes on a more detrimental aspect that  leads to the formation of noise peaks &#10;in the spectrum and to a chaotic behaviour in the time domain. This more violent degradation is what is called MI.&#10;&#10;Clearly, MI and SPM occur together, so it is difficult to detect which one is prevailing. &#10;However, if the signal is set to CW, SPM disappears, whereas MI does not. &#10;So one easy check is to use a CW laser and see what happens.&#10;Therefore we also enclosed another example, in this same directory, called mi800cw.opf, where modulation &#10;is suppressed. Otherwise, all other propagation aspects are unchanged. &#10;&#10;If you are interested in PG ad MI, go to OptSim's on-line help and look for the bibliography section. &#10;Most of the papers listed in there  are indeed about PG. OptSim was extensively used to check analytical and&#10;experimental results, and the perfect adherence of simulations to  analysis and experiments has also turned out &#10;to be be a strong validation of OptSim as a reliable simulator.    </doc>
